<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#direct-democracy">Glossary</a> · Term · government</p><h1 class="enc-title">Direct democracy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">508 BCE – present</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Direct democracy is government by the citizens themselves rather than by their representatives. Classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, which ruled itself through an assembly, a council and juries chosen by lot from 508 to 322 BCE, gave it its name and its first defenders and critics; today it lives on in the Swiss open-air assemblies and in the referendums and initiatives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, many US states and other countries.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Confederacy's institutions, the Landsgemeinde, cantonal sovereignty and consensus through the Diet, remain the historical roots of the federalism and <b>direct democracy</b> of modern Switzerland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…so that a great Austrian noble family could sit in the Imperial Diet, outlived the Holy Roman Empire, the German Confederation and the Habsburg monarchy to become a sovereign microstate that combines a powerful hereditary monarchy with <b>direct democracy</b> and one of the world’s highest incomes per head.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-liechtenstein">Principality of Liechtenstein · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Created by Napoleon's Act of Mediation in 1803 and remade as a federal state in 1848, the Swiss Confederation became a model of permanent neutrality, federalism and <b>direct democracy</b>, and through Geneva and the Red Cross a centre of international humanitarian law and diplomacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The American founders often equated <b>direct democracy</b> with mob rule, Thomas Jefferson warned that “an elective despotism is not the government we fought for”, and Richard Ellis and Michael Nelson argue that much constitutional thought from Madison to Lincoln turned on “the problem of majority tyranny”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/republicanism">Republicanism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Sovereignty lay with the communes, which voted on major decisions, an early form of <b>direct democracy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/three-leagues">Three Leagues</a></li></ul></section>
